```json
{
  "root_causes": [
    {
      "service": "ts-payment-service",
      "fault_kind": "pod_failure",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "kind": "trace",
          "sql": "SELECT date_trunc('minute', time) m, service_name, span_name, count(*) n, round(avg(duration)/1e6,1) avg_ms, sum(CASE WHEN attr_status_code='Error' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) errs FROM abnormal_traces WHERE (service_name='ts-payment-service' AND span_name='PaymentController.pay') OR (service_name='ts-inside-payment-service' AND span_name='POST /api/v1/inside_pay_service/inside_payment') GROUP BY 1,2,3 ORDER BY 1,2,3",
          "claim": "During 09:25 inside-payment's pay requests error (19 of 25) while payment's pay spans are sparse and slow; both recover by 09:26."
        },
        {
          "kind": "trace",
          "sql": "SELECT service_name, span_name, attr_http_response_status_code code, count(*) n, round(avg(duration)/1e6,1) avg_ms FROM abnormal_traces WHERE attr_status_code='Error' GROUP BY 1,2,3 ORDER BY n DESC",
          "claim": "Failed inside-payment outgoing POSTs return 503 after about 3.6-4.6 s, with no matching payment server span in those traces."
        },
        {
          "kind": "metric",
          "sql": "SELECT date_trunc('minute', time) m, service_name, metric, round(avg(value),3) v FROM abnormal_metrics WHERE service_name='ts-payment-service' AND metric IN ('container.cpu.usage','container.memory.working_set','k8s.pod.memory.page_faults') GROUP BY 1,2,3 ORDER BY 3,1",
          "claim": "Payment pod CPU spikes to about 2 cores at 09:25 and falls back to idle by 09:27, with a working-set dip in the same minute."
        },
        {
          "kind": "log",
          "sql": "SELECT date_trunc('minute', time) m, count(*) n FROM abnormal_logs WHERE service_name='mysql' AND message LIKE '%Aborted connection%' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1",
          "claim": "MySQL logs 10 aborted client connections in the 09:25 minute, consistent with a dropped payment-side connection pool; none appear in normal logs."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "propagation": [
    {
      "from": "ts-payment-service",
      "to": "ts-inside-payment-service",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "kind": "trace",
          "sql": "SELECT service_name, span_name, attr_status_code, attr_http_response_status_code code, count(*) n, round(avg(duration)/1e6,1) avg_ms FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='ts-inside-payment-service' AND span_name IN ('POST','InsidePaymentController.pay') AND attr_status_code='Error' GROUP BY 1,2,3,4",
          "claim": "Inside-payment's outgoing POST calls to payment fail with 503 after several seconds, and its pay handler errors in turn."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "from": "ts-inside-payment-service",
      "to": "ts-ui-dashboard",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "kind": "trace",
          "sql": "SELECT service_name, span_name, attr_status_code, attr_http_response_status_code code, count(*) n, round(avg(duration)/1e6,1) avg_ms FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='ts-ui-dashboard' AND span_name='POST /api/v1/inside_pay_service/inside_payment' GROUP BY 1,2,3,4",
          "claim": "Dashboard's inside_payment POST spans take about 2.9 s on average in the abnormal window, carrying inside-payment's 500 failures to users."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

The degradation is confined to the inside-payment flow (`/api/v1/inside_pay_service/inside_payment`). The failure starts with `ts-payment-service` and reaches users through `ts-inside-payment-service` and `ts-ui-dashboard`. The fault kind is the least certain part. The `pod_failure` label rests on a brief CPU burst and working-set dip in the payment pod, mysql aborted connections, and failed calls with no payment-side server span. The data has no restart counter or uptime metric to confirm a restart, and the CPU burst could also point to a saturation kind. I did not name any other fault: the other services' own metrics and error shares show no other change that propagated to users. The hubble flow increase between inside-payment and payment showed no drops, so I did not treat it as a network fault.

